From: dappy <dappymint@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winemac: host cross-process metal layers over CAContext

A process rendering into another process's window cannot reach that window's
Cocoa views, so it renders into an offscreen layer tree, publishes it over a
CAContext, and the owning process hosts it with a CALayerHost. Chromium's gpu
process renders into exactly such a window, which is why steam's ui needs it.

Replaces patches 0003, 0013, 0016 and 0017, which were four iterations of this
against wine 10. Wine 11 fixed half the problem on its own:
macdrv_client_surface_create now builds a standalone view and no longer needs
get_win_data(hwnd) to succeed, which was the specific bail-out 0003 existed
for. So the surface gets created, dxvk and MoltenVK render into it, and every
old failure signature reads zero.

Attachment is what is still missing, and it is why steam was still black on a
wine 11 runtime built with these held out. Measured 2026-08-13 on 4.3.81: 103
swapchains created, 57 distinct WineMetalView layers at real geometry
(1800x988, 1278x657, 690x744, and 700x440 for the login window), and the window
reading luma 0.0 with one colour at t=270s, well past the ~210s steam takes to
paint. Correct frames, produced and going nowhere, because
macdrv_client_surface_update resolves the toplevel through get_win_data and
returns early when another process owns it.

Hooking client_surface rather than vulkan.c is what makes this one patch
instead of four: update() runs on every vkQueuePresentKHR, on the thread doing
the present, which is a wine thread and so may post (0016 was written because
the old code posted from the cocoa main thread and the post never took effect).
The same hook serves opengl, vulkan and d3dmetal, where the wine 10 version had
to touch vulkan.c and d3dmetal.c separately.

Two things carried over from the wine 10 iterations, both measured then:
CALayerHost takes its size from the published tree and ignores the frame the
hosting side sets, so the sender positions the drawable inside a container
sized to the top level's client area; and the receiving side must size its view
from user32, because the cached rects are empty for a child window the driver
never draws.

d3dmetal declines the cross-process case rather than being handed a null view.
Steam's ui goes through dxvk, so that path is not needed for it.
---
diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
index ffb7c6af..52b01b5a 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
@@ -46,6 +46,25 @@ - (void)_setPreventsActivation:(BOOL)flag;
 
 @end
 
+typedef uint32_t CGSConnectionID;
+extern CGSConnectionID CGSMainConnectionID(void);
+
+typedef uint32_t CAContextID;
+
+@interface CAContext : NSObject
+
++ (id) contextWithCGSConnection:(CGSConnectionID)connection options:(NSDictionary*)options;
+@property(readonly) CAContextID contextId;
+@property(retain) CALayer* layer;
+
+@end
+
+@interface CALayerHost : CALayer
+
+- (void) setContextId:(CAContextID)contextId;
+
+@end
+
 
